  "command": "LEAN_BRIDGE_COMPOSER='/usr/bin/composer' LEAN_BRIDGE_NATIVE_TEST_GLIBC_FLOOR='2.36' LEAN_BRIDGE_NOMINAL_SUBTYPE_PHP_COMPOSER_VERSION='2.5.5' LEAN_BRIDGE_NOMINAL_SUBTYPE_PHP_MODES='full' LEAN_BRIDGE_NOMINAL_SUBTYPE_PHP_OUTPUT='/app/build/vo1466-nominal-php-installed-ordinary-full-r1-output' LEAN_BRIDGE_NOMINAL_SUBTYPE_PHP_ROUTES='ordinary' LEAN_BRIDGE_NOMINAL_SUBTYPE_PHP_TEST='1' LEAN_BRIDGE_NOMINAL_SUBTYPE_PHP_VERSION='8.2.33' LEAN_BRIDGE_PHP='/usr/bin/php' LEAN_NUM_THREADS='1' MAKEFLAGS='-j1' NO_COLOR='1' OMP_NUM_THREADS='1' '/usr/bin/node' '--test' '--test-concurrency=1' '--test-reporter=tap' 'tests/helpers/nominal-subtype-php-installed-tests.mjs'",
  "scope": "Two independent author roots reproduce the complete Composer ZIP. Author and build roots are removed before offline installation. Unchanged weak and strict PHP callers replay after moving the installed consumer tree, for four executions per selection. Consumer PHP source remains present. The full fixture checks per-use constructor selection and normalization, repeated record fields, active variant branches, aliases, mixed Fin guards, exact rejection paths, caller-input preservation and recovery. Nominal acceptance covers the tested Nat Subtype records, variants, aliases and copied constructor-free results with PHP 8.2.33 and Composer 2.5.5 through offline Composer installation on Linux x64/glibc 2.36 with an explicit 2.36 package floor. Weak and strict consumers run after author/build removal and again after relocation. PHP consumer source remains required at runtime. Other primitive-base nominal fixtures and PHP-Wasm need separate acceptance. Refined callbacks, owned, recursive, dependent and broader generic Subtypes remain pending. Constructor/source entry counts and memory are unmeasured; these local runs do not establish hosted release-floor acceptance. The repository archive retains all non-native ZIP members, Composer autoload inputs, receipts and source snapshots, but not native binaries or complete ZIPs. The complete PHP and Composer distributions remain trusted tools.",
